  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sanjay Bhutra vs State Of Raj And Anr on 5 December, 2018 High Court Of Judicature For Rajasthan Bench At Jaipur S.B. Criminal Miscellaneous (Petition) No. 6148/2017 Sanjay Bhutra ----Petitioner Versus State Of Raj And Anr ----Respondent For Petitioner(s) : Mr. Gd Bansal with Mr. Ashwani Gupta For Respondent(s) : Mr. Prakash Thakuriya Pp Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Kanwaljit Singh Ahluwalia -/Order/- 05/12/2018",
    "facts": "",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner has stated at Bar that on 1.6.2010 sample of Ghee from the shop of petitioner was drawn by Food Inspector. Learned counsel for the petitioner has contended that the State Food Laboratory vide report dated 24.6.2010 found the sample to be adulterated. Learned counsel for the petitioner has contended that eleven months later on 21.5.2011 Health Authority i.e. Chief Medical and Health Officer ordered prosecution of the petitioner. It is submitted that the complaint was filed on 14.6.2011 almost after one year. Learned counsel for the petitioner has submitted that shelf life of Ghee is six months. It is submitted that on 17.7.2011 petitioner received notice under Section 13(2) of Prevention of Food Adulteration Act. It is contended that within ten days on 23.7.2011 petitioner filed an application for sending the second sample to the Central Food Laboratory. Learned counsel for the petitioner has contended that even though the court passed the order, the second sample was (2 of 2) [CRLMP-6148/2017] produced in the court on 2.9.2017 after delay of more than seven years. Issue notice to the respondents for 4.2.2019.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner has stated at Bar that on 1.6.2010 sample of Ghee from the shop of petitioner was drawn by Food Inspector. Learned counsel for the petitioner has contended that the State Food Laboratory vide report dated 24.6.2010 found the sample to be adulterated. Learned counsel for the petitioner has contended that eleven months later on 21.5.2011 Health Authority i.e. Chief Medical and Health Officer ordered prosecution of the petitioner. It is submitted that the complaint was filed on 14.6.2011 almost after one year. Learned counsel for the petitioner has submitted that shelf life of Ghee is six months. It is submitted that on 17.7.2011 petitioner received notice under Section 13(2) of Prevention of Food Adulteration Act. It is contended that within ten days on 23.7.2011 petitioner filed an application for sending the second sample to the Central Food Laboratory. Learned counsel for the petitioner has contended that even though the court passed the order, the second sample was (2 of 2) [CRLMP-6148/2017] produced in the court on 2.9.2017 after delay of more than seven years. Issue notice to the respondents for 4.2.2019.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
